17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 6–7, 1864","place":"Orange Plank Road, Brock Road, Orange Turnpike, and the Wilderness of Spotsylvania, Virginia","result":"Longstreet’s flank attack drove Hancock’s II Corps back to the Brock Road defenses, but Confederate friendly fire then disabled Longstreet and halted the attack. Lee’s later frontal assault failed. Gordon’s evening attack broke two U.S. brigades but came too late and with too few troops to change the result. Grant treated the battle as a draw and ordered the army southeast toward Spotsylvania Court House","facts":[{"label":"Longstreet’s flanking column","value":"4 brigades"},{"label":"Longstreet wounded","value":"About noon, May 6"},{"label":"Lee’s renewed assault","value":"After 4 p.m."},{"label":"Gordon’s assault force","value":"About 4,000 men in 3 brigades"},{"label":"Gordon’s main attack","value":"Near sunset"},{"label":"Second Gordon attack","value":"About 10 p.m."},{"label":"Grant’s Spotsylvania order","value":"6:30 a.m., May 7"},{"label":"Battle result","value":"Tactical draw; campaign continued south"}],"maps":[{"title":"Longstreet’s flank attack, 9 a.m.–noon","caption":"The National Park Service map shows four Confederate brigades moving east along the unfinished railroad and striking the exposed left of Hancock’s II Corps. Blue arrows show the U.S. retreat toward Brock Road.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=b0583427-a351-4929-80f1-54ced0bdc400","image":"/images/wilderness-may6-9am-noon-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from 9 a.m. to noon on May 6, 1864"},{"title":"Lee’s afternoon attacks, noon–5 p.m.","caption":"This map shows Hancock’s reorganized line along Brock Road and Lee’s unsuccessful frontal attacks after Longstreet was wounded.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=5aaa75e9-5f8f-4bd0-bbde-b821ea9b5d91","image":"/images/wilderness-may6-noon-5pm-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from noon to 5 p.m. on May 6, 1864"},{"title":"Gordon’s evening flank attack, 5–9 p.m.","caption":"The final map shows Gordon, Johnston, and Hoffman moving against the exposed northern end of Sedgwick’s VI Corps. The attack captured two U.S. brigade commanders but stopped after Federal reinforcements formed a new line.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=22352330-0187-41b6-ab01-388404a11750","image":"/images/wilderness-may6-5pm-9pm-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from 5 to 9 p.m. on May 6, 1864"},{"title":"The battlefield today","caption":"The modern map centers on the Orange Plank Road and Brock Road intersection. The Orange Turnpike fighting occurred to the north, and the unfinished railroad used by Longstreet ran west of Brock Road.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.79%2C38.27%2C-77.66%2C38.36&layer=mapnik&marker=38.2968%2C-77.7442"},{"title":"The Wilderness in the Overland Campaign","caption":"The campaign map places the battle west of Fredericksburg and shows the next movement southeast to Spotsylvania Court House.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign from the Wilderness toward Spotsylvania"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:26","audioTime":86,"title":"Longstreet prepared a flank attack","detail":"Martin L. Smith found an unfinished railroad south of Orange Plank Road that led toward the unguarded left of Hancock’s II Corps."},{"time":"2:07","audioTime":127,"title":"Sorrel received four brigades","detail":"Longstreet ordered G. Moxley Sorrel to collect four scattered brigades, move them along the railroad bed, and wait until all were ready before attacking."},{"time":"3:11","audioTime":191,"title":"The attack struck Hancock’s left","detail":"The Confederates came from the south against U.S. troops facing west. Two brigades on the exposed flank broke, uncovering the units nearer Orange Plank Road."},{"time":"4:46","audioTime":286,"title":"Longstreet attacked from the front","detail":"Troops astride Orange Plank Road advanced as the flanking column hit, forcing Hancock’s command back toward the Brock Road intersection."},{"time":"5:44","audioTime":344,"title":"II Corps rallied behind fieldworks","detail":"Soldiers stopped at the Brock Road defenses. Officers and color bearers reorganized the units in roughly half an hour."},{"time":"6:20","audioTime":380,"title":"Friendly fire stopped the Confederate advance","detail":"The pressure eased after soldiers of the 12th Virginia mistook Longstreet’s mounted party for U.S. troops and fired into it."},{"time":"7:00","audioTime":420,"title":"Micah Jenkins was killed and Longstreet wounded","detail":"The volley killed Jenkins, a staff officer, and a courier. A bullet passed through Longstreet’s shoulder and injured his neck."},{"time":"7:37","audioTime":457,"title":"Longstreet left the field for months","detail":"Surgeons stopped the bleeding, but he could not return to the Army of Northern Virginia until October 1864."},{"time":"8:16","audioTime":496,"title":"Lee resumed the attack after 4 p.m.","detail":"After taking control of Longstreet’s corps, Lee ordered a frontal assault on the Brock Road defenses."},{"time":"8:33","audioTime":513,"title":"The frontal assault failed","detail":"Confederates entered the works at a few points but were driven out by U.S. musket and artillery fire."},{"time":"9:19","audioTime":559,"title":"Hancock canceled the planned U.S. attack","detail":"His troops were exhausted and short of ammunition after stopping Lee’s assault, so a proposed 6 p.m. advance did not occur."},{"time":"9:34","audioTime":574,"title":"Gordon proposed a northern flank attack","detail":"John B. Gordon identified the exposed end of Sedgwick’s VI Corps above the Orange Turnpike and sought permission to attack it."},{"time":"10:31","audioTime":631,"title":"Scouts confirmed the U.S. flank was open","detail":"Gordon’s scouts found no U.S. line opposite his brigade and reported that the Federal works ended farther south."},{"time":"11:31","audioTime":691,"title":"Gordon personally checked the position","detail":"He approached close enough to see U.S. soldiers at the end of their breastworks and confirmed that no line protected their flank."},{"time":"12:48","audioTime":768,"title":"Early opposed the proposal","detail":"Jubal Early argued that Gordon lacked enough men and might be outflanked by Burnside or other U.S. forces reported nearby."},{"time":"14:16","audioTime":856,"title":"Ewell initially refused permission","detail":"Richard Ewell accepted Early’s objections and delayed the attack until the situation became clearer."},{"time":"14:36","audioTime":876,"title":"Robert Johnston’s brigade arrived","detail":"Johnston extended the Confederate left beyond the U.S. flank. Reports also established that Burnside was not positioned behind Sedgwick."},{"time":"15:15","audioTime":915,"title":"Three Confederate brigades moved at sunset","detail":"Gordon attacked with his own brigade, Johnston’s brigade, and John Hoffman’s brigade—about 4,000 men in total."},{"time":"18:10","audioTime":1090,"title":"Shaler’s brigade collapsed","detail":"The surprise attack struck Alexander Shaler’s brigade first, then forced Truman Seymour’s neighboring brigade to withdraw."},{"time":"19:47","audioTime":1187,"title":"Shaler and Seymour were captured","detail":"Both brigade commanders rode toward the fighting and attempted to rally their troops before Confederates took them prisoner."},{"time":"20:00","audioTime":1200,"title":"The attack did not change the battle","detail":"Only Gordon’s brigade struck the flank directly. Dense woods and darkness disrupted the other two brigades while Sedgwick formed a new defensive line."},{"time":"20:36","audioTime":1236,"title":"A second attack failed","detail":"Gordon tried again around 10 p.m., but the reinforced U.S. position stopped the advance."},{"time":"22:48","audioTime":1368,"title":"Grant assessed the battle as a draw","detail":"He concluded that neither army had won a clear advantage and rejected another attack against prepared Confederate fieldworks."},{"time":"24:04","audioTime":1444,"title":"Grant ordered a march to Spotsylvania","detail":"At 6:30 a.m. on May 7, he directed Meade to prepare the Army of the Potomac for a night movement southeast around Lee’s right."},{"time":"25:44","audioTime":1544,"title":"U.S. soldiers saw Grant riding south","detail":"Troops along Brock Road understood that the army was continuing the campaign instead of withdrawing north and cheered as Grant passed."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Directed the campaign and ordered the movement to Spotsylvania"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Issued army orders under Grant’s strategic direction"},{"commander":"Winfield Scott Hancock","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Defended the Orange Plank Road and Brock Road intersection"},{"commander":"John Sedgwick","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Held the U.S. right above the Orange Turnpike"},{"commander":"Truman Seymour and Alexander Shaler","command":"VI Corps brigades","subordinates":"Occupied the exposed northern flank; both were captured"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Took control of Longstreet’s corps after Longstreet was wounded"},{"commander":"James Longstreet","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"Coordinated the morning flank and frontal attacks before friendly fire wounded him"},{"commander":"G. Moxley Sorrel","command":"Longstreet’s chief of staff","subordinates":"Led four brigades along the unfinished railroad"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Controlled the Confederate line north of Orange Turnpike"},{"commander":"Jubal A. Early","command":"Ewell’s division","subordinates":"Initially opposed Gordon’s proposed flank attack"},{"commander":"John B. Gordon","command":"Early’s brigade","subordinates":"Led the sunset attack with Johnston and Hoffman"}]}],"people":[{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Planned the successful morning flank attack and was then severely wounded by Confederate fire","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"G. Moxley Sorrel","role":"Organized and led Longstreet’s four-brigade flanking column","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John B. Gordon","role":"Found the open U.S. right flank and led the evening assault","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Winfield Scott Hancock","role":"Rallied II Corps at the Brock Road works after its left was driven in","side":"United States","image":"/images/hancock-division-commanders.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670736/"},{"name":"John Sedgwick","role":"Reinforced the exposed VI Corps flank after Gordon’s attack","side":"United States","image":"/images/john-sedgwick.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666485/"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Decided against another Wilderness attack and ordered the army toward Spotsylvania"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Resumed the afternoon assault after Longstreet left the field","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"losses":[{"name":"Micah Jenkins","side":"Confederacy","role":"Brigade commander in Longstreet’s corps","outcome":"Killed by Confederate friendly fire on Orange Plank Road"},{"name":"James Longstreet","side":"Confederacy","role":"First Corps commander","outcome":"Severely wounded by Confederate friendly fire; absent until October 1864"},{"name":"Alexander Shaler","side":"United States","role":"VI Corps brigade commander","outcome":"Captured during Gordon’s evening attack"},{"name":"Truman Seymour","side":"United States","role":"VI Corps brigade commander","outcome":"Captured during Gordon’s evening attack"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/wilderness-may6-9am-noon-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of Longstreet’s May 6 flank attack","caption":"The morning map shows why the unfinished railroad created a usable approach to Hancock’s open left flank.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=b0583427-a351-4929-80f1-54ced0bdc400"},{"image":"/images/hancock-division-commanders.jpg","alt":"Winfield Scott Hancock with three II Corps division commanders","caption":"Hancock rallied II Corps behind its Brock Road fieldworks after Longstreet’s attack drove it from the woods.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670736/"},{"image":"/images/ulysses-s-grant.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Ulysses S. Grant","caption":"Grant chose to continue southeast toward Spotsylvania rather than withdraw across the Rapidan.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Robert E. Lee","caption":"Lee took personal control of First Corps after Longstreet was wounded and ordered the unsuccessful afternoon assault.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 483","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/02/02/civil-war-podcast-episode-483/"},{"label":"National Park Service: History of the Battle of the Wilderness","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/learn/historyculture/wildspot.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Wilderness troop movement maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery.htm?id=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Overland Campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Ulysses S. Grant portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert E. Lee portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Hancock and division commanders","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670736/"}]}}]}]
